🔍 Sequence Detector Using Mealy FSM

FSM-Based Binary Sequence Detection System using Verilog HDL

📌 Project Overview

This project implements a binary sequence detector using a Finite State Machine (FSM) in Verilog HDL.

The system is designed to detect the binary sequence 1011 using a Mealy state machine. The detector monitors serial input data and generates an output signal HIGH whenever the specified sequence is detected.

The design supports overlapping sequence detection, allowing the system to identify multiple occurrences of the sequence in continuous input data.

The project is developed using Verilog HDL and is intended for FPGA implementation using Xilinx Artix-7 architecture.

✨ Features

- FSM-based sequence detector design
- Mealy state machine implementation
- Binary sequence detection (1011)
- Serial data input processing
- Overlapping sequence detection
- Synchronous reset functionality
- Single-bit detection output
- Verilog testbench for functional verification
- FPGA implementation using Xilinx Vivado

🛠️ Hardware Requirements

- FPGA development board (Artix-7)
- Clock input
- Reset switch
- Serial data input switch
- Output LED for sequence detection
- USB programming cable

💻 Software Requirements

- Xilinx Vivado Design Suite
- Verilog HDL
- VS Code / Notepad++
- GitHub for source code management

⚙️ Working Principle

The sequence detector uses four FSM states to identify the binary pattern 1011.

State| Description
S0| Initial state
S1| Detected 1
S2| Detected 10
S3| Detected 101

When the input sequence reaches 1011, the output signal becomes HIGH, indicating successful sequence detection.

The Mealy FSM generates the output based on the current state and input.

